course: dictionary
lesson: 14232
title: Nastiness and unkind behaviour
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nastiness is a noun, a name for a thing. It means unkind, mean or spiteful behaviour. «The new girl was upset by the nastiness of the other children.» Here, nastiness is the mean way they treated her.
explain: The word comes from nasty, which is the adjective. When someone is nasty, they show nastiness. Nastiness can also describe how unpleasant something is. «The nastiness of the smell from the bin made us hold our noses.»
explain: Use context clues to find the meaning. Words like upset, hold our noses and spiteful tell you the word is about bad behaviour or bad feelings. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the unkind behaviour of the other children made her upset" still makes sense, so the meaning fits.
fact: Nastiness is a noun: a word for a quality or a type of behaviour.
fact: «Nastiness» means unkind, mean or spiteful behaviour.
fact: «Nastiness» can also mean being very unpleasant, like the nastiness of a smell or an illness.
fact: Spiteful means deliberately trying to hurt someone.
fact: Nastiness is usually an uncountable noun, so we say «some nastiness», not «a nastiness».
exercise: «There's no need for nastiness in our playground.» What does nastiness mean here? | *Unkind, mean or spiteful behaviour | Happy games and laughter | A type of sweet | Loud singing
exercise: «The nastiness of the sour milk made me gag.» What does nastiness mean here? | A new hobby | Kindness to others | Being very tired | *Being very unpleasant, like a bad smell
exercise: Which word is the noun that means mean behaviour? | nasty | nastily | *nastiness | nastier
test: «Her nastiness towards her little brother upset Mum.» What does nastiness mean here? | Kindness and care | Loud laughter | *Unkind, mean behaviour | Shyness
test: Which sentence uses nastiness to mean being very unpleasant? | *The nastiness of the illness kept Sam in bed for a week. | Our nastiness was a warm, soft blanket. | We played a nastiness game at break.
exam: Ben said, «There's no need for nastiness, Sophie.» What is Ben asking Sophie to stop? | Running fast | Being quiet | Being hungry | *Being unkind or spiteful
